Store the tunnel parameters whatever port the UE registered from.

ipsec_create() only hands the ipsec_t it just built to the contact when the
REGISTER's Via sent-by port is 5060. Nothing requires a UE to source its
initial, unprotected REGISTER from 5060 — RFC 3261 does not, and TS 33.203 only
fixes the ports for the protected messages — so a UE that puts its own port in
the Via keeps whatever save_pending() stored from the Security-Client header.
That struct has the UE's spi_uc/spi_us but zero for spi_pc/spi_ps/port_pc/
port_ps, because those are only chosen later, by acquire_spi() here.

The tunnel is still created correctly (create_ipsec_tunnel() uses the local `s`),
but the contact ends up describing it wrongly, and destroy_ipsec_tunnel() at
expiry reads the contact. It removes the two outbound SAs, whose keys come from
the UE's own SPIs, and silently fails to remove the two inbound ones, which are
keyed by spi_pc/spi_ps == 0. Half the tunnel is left installed in the kernel on
every ended registration.

Storing is not conditional on anything: at this point create_ipsec_tunnel() has
already succeeded, so the contact should describe what was created. Dropping the
guard also stops req_sec_params->data.ipsec from being freed on the cleanup path
in this case, which is right — it is now owned by the contact.
